<section class="page-hero page-hero--paper"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Licensing</p><h1>Commercially licensed. Free to evaluate.</h1><p class="page-hero__lede">PicoFlow is not open source, and we would rather say so on this page than after you have built a proof of concept. Personal evaluation costs nothing and takes one email. Production deployments are licensed per application.</p></div></section>
<section class="section section--white"><div class="shell license-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Getting a key</p><h2 class="section-heading">Request an evaluation key; use it for as long as the evaluation takes.</h2><p class="section-lede">Evaluation keys are issued on request and returned the same working day. Verification is entirely offline: the runtime checks the token's Ed25519 signature against a public key compiled into the package on the first model call, then caches the result. There is no licence server, no network request, and no telemetry.</p><div class="hero__actions"><a class="button button--ink" href="mailto:{{ site.email }}?subject=PicoFlow%20evaluation%20key">Request an evaluation key</a><a class="button button--outline" href="mailto:{{ site.email }}?subject=PicoFlow%20commercial%20licence">Ask about commercial terms</a></div></div><div class="license-list"><article><h3>Personal evaluation — free</h3><p>Individual learning, proof-of-concept work, and first-flow experimentation. No commitment and no card.</p></article><article><h3>Production — per application</h3><p>Deployment guidance, version support, and commercial runtime terms. Priced against the deployment rather than seat count; contact us for a quote.</p></article><article><h3>If the licence is a blocker</h3><p>Some organisations require permissively licensed dependencies. If that is you, <a href="/compare/langgraph/">direct LangGraph</a> is the honest recommendation and the comparison page explains the trade-off.</p></article></div></div></section>
